Yes. For production, I’d stop hard-coding the UAT load balancer and make the target workers explicit so the same JAR can stress:

cdp-p-wrkr1.hodomain.local
cdp-p-wrkr2.hodomain.local
cdp-p-wrkr3.hodomain.local

A clean approach is to keep workerNodeId and build the hostname from it:

String host = "cdp-p-wrkr" + workerNodeId + ".hodomain.local";

Then your JDBC URL becomes:

String jdbcUrl =
        "jdbc:impala://" + host + ":21050/DEV_CNSMR_LO" +
        ";AuthMech=1" +
        ";KrbAuthType=2" +
        ";KrbRealm=HODOMAIN.LOCAL" +
        ";KrbHostFQDN=" + host +
        ";KrbServiceName=impala" +
        ";SSL=1" +
        ";SSLTrustStore=/home/rise37432/ImpalaStressTest/securityConfigs/cm-auto-global_truststore.jks" +
        ";SSLTrustStorePwd=CDPEIMPASS" +
        ";UseNativeQuery=1;";

So:

new ImpalaService(1)

targets:

cdp-p-wrkr1.hodomain.local

and:

new ImpalaService(2)

targets:

cdp-p-wrkr2.hodomain.local

and:

new ImpalaService(3)

targets:

cdp-p-wrkr3.hodomain.local

I’d change your constructor to this:

public ImpalaService(int workerNodeId) {

    if (workerNodeId < 1 || workerNodeId > 3) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "workerNodeId must be 1, 2, or 3"
        );
    }

    String host =
            "cdp-p-wrkr" + workerNodeId + ".hodomain.local";

    String jdbcUrl =
            "jdbc:impala://" + host + ":21050/DEV_CNSMR_LO" +
            ";AuthMech=1" +
            ";KrbAuthType=2" +
            ";KrbRealm=HODOMAIN.LOCAL" +
            ";KrbHostFQDN=" + host +
            ";KrbServiceName=impala" +
            ";SSL=1" +
            ";SSLTrustStore=/home/rise37432/ImpalaStressTest/securityConfigs/cm-auto-global_truststore.jks" +
            ";SSLTrustStorePwd=CDPEIMPASS" +
            ";UseNativeQuery=1;";

    System.out.println("========================================");
    System.out.println("PRODUCTION IMPALA STRESS TEST");
    System.out.println("Worker       = " + workerNodeId);
    System.out.println("Impala host  = " + host);
    System.out.println("JDBC URL     = " + jdbcUrl);
    System.out.println("========================================");

    HikariConfig readConfig =
            createBaseConfig(jdbcUrl);

    readConfig.setPoolName(
            "HikariPool-Read-wrkr" + workerNodeId
    );

    readConfig.setMaximumPoolSize(50);
    readConfig.setConnectionTimeout(10000);

    this.readDataSource =
            new HikariDataSource(readConfig);

    HikariConfig writeConfig =
            createBaseConfig(jdbcUrl);

    writeConfig.setPoolName(
            "HikariPool-Write-wrkr" + workerNodeId
    );

    writeConfig.setMaximumPoolSize(14);
    writeConfig.setConnectionTimeout(10000);

    this.writeDataSource =
            new HikariDataSource(writeConfig);
}

Because you're using:

KrbAuthType=2

remember to prepare the production Kerberos ticket before starting the JAR:

export KRB5_CONFIG=/home/rise37432/ImpalaStressTest/securityConfigs/kerb5.ini
export KRB5CCNAME=FILE:/tmp/krb5cc_$(id -u)

kinit -kt \
/home/rise37432/ImpalaStressTest/securityConfigs/rise37432.keytab \
rise37432@HODOMAIN.LOCAL

klist

Then verify each production worker before stressing it:

nc -vz cdp-p-wrkr1.hodomain.local 21050
nc -vz cdp-p-wrkr2.hodomain.local 21050
nc -vz cdp-p-wrkr3.hodomain.local 21050

I would also obtain the service tickets once to verify Kerberos:

kvno impala/cdp-p-wrkr1.hodomain.local@HODOMAIN.LOCAL
kvno impala/cdp-p-wrkr2.hodomain.local@HODOMAIN.LOCAL
kvno impala/cdp-p-wrkr3.hodomain.local@HODOMAIN.LOCAL

For production, I strongly recommend starting with a deliberately low stress level—e.g. 5–10 concurrent queries, then 20, 30, etc.—while watching the Impala metrics. Don't immediately reuse a UAT setting such as 50 read + 14 write connections against production.

Also, for the Prometheus pilot we planned, these same three hosts become your production scrape targets:

static_configs:
  - targets:
      - "cdp-p-wrkr1.hodomain.local:25000"
    labels:
      worker: "wrkr1"

  - targets:
      - "cdp-p-wrkr2.hodomain.local:25000"
    labels:
      worker: "wrkr2"

  - targets:
      - "cdp-p-wrkr3.hodomain.local:25000"
    labels:
      worker: "wrkr3"

One architectural choice matters: if the goal is to test production load-balancer distribution, you should keep JDBC pointed at the production LB and monitor these three workers. If the goal is to benchmark each worker individually, direct JDBC to wrkr1, wrkr2, and wrkr3 as above. Those are two different tests.
